When we were growing up,

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.170
We had Bible reading and prayer.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.640
every night about eight o'clock.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.850
And mother would read the scripture.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.260
And Daddy would always pray.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.340
That was a habit just like brushing our teeth, and that's what we did.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.010
We never thought about did we enjoy it.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:34.700
We just did it.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.780
Billy Graham was born in 1918.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:44.130
His parents had a dairy farm in North Carolina.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.840
And his parents were conservative Presbyterians.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:59.100
His family life was very much like that of a great many other people in Mecklenburg County, North Carolina.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:06.870
They believed in God, and most of them believed that God wanted them to win souls,

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.860
to evangelize other people, to bring them to Christ.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.060
Young Billy Frank Graham, as he was known at the time,

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.510
was in some ways a normal teenager.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.470
He was rebelling to some degree

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.390
against the strict piety of his parents,

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:27.100
although not overtly so.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:29.840
After he got a...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.360
he had the advantage of being able to borrow his father's car

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.220
and spend luxurious nights with girls,

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.880
parking, going to movies.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.510
He really liked the girls, and they liked him.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:44.970
Even though he had a head...

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.780
full of scriptures and prayers.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.820
Billy wasn't completely convinced

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:51.380
that he was a true Christian yet.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.920
One night, he and his friends

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.950
went to hear a traveling revivalist

00:07:58.950 --> 00:07:59.850
coming through the area.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.520
named Mordecai Ham.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.920
You are not acceptable in heaven.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.180
Do you want to know it before it's too late?

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:11.340
How many of you do?

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:11.760
I do.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.700
Lift your hand right now.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:14.960
At some point during that...

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:22.480
At that gathering, something Ham said connected with the young Billy Frank Graham, and he

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.850
decided at that moment to embrace the religion of his parents.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.220
Billy wanted to go to college at the University of North Carolina.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:37.370
But his mother had come to believe that

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:41.110
The road to hell went right through the campus of state schools.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:45.910
So she wanted him to go, as did his father, to a serious Christian college.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:52.420
He was sent off to Bob Jones College, which was one of the strictest fundamentalist colleges.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.120
Prior to

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:56.700
the late 19th century.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.990
Baptists, Methodists, and Presbyterians believed in

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.060
and the authority of scripture.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:05.210
They believe that Jesus died for their sins and they believe that

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.930
They needed to convert.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:07.810
The masses.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:10.230
You had cultural.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.900
transformation and intellectual developments

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.520
that began to disrupt.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:16.460
Protestant culture.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.090
Protestant fundamentalism is a religious movement.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.720
It's a religious response to everything.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.440
we associate with modernity.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.070
Technology, urbanization, the rise of sciences.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.230
All of a sudden, Protestants were broken up

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.030
into multiple camps, and fundamentalists

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.610
were just those who dug their heels.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.960
They drew a line in the sand.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:57.660
They said, the Bible says it, I believe it, that settles it.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.320
We maintain the glorious idea that the gospel does not change.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.600
It isn't reinterpreted from one generation to another generation.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:13.920
In fundamentalist circles,

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:15.600
The Bible was...

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.940
dictated by God directly to human agents who wrote it down without error.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:22.420
and passed it along.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.790
and with his fully dependable word.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:29.980
And so when they're looking at the book of Genesis and they see that God created.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.880
the world in six days and rested on the seventh, they're taking those as literal.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:35.580
days.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.600
Protestant fundamentalists are driven by a particular urgency.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:47.100
because they expect the world to come to an end very soon.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.840
It's really not worth it to get involved in politics or culture.

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:59.910
They want to separate themselves from the world to preserve the fundamentals of the faith.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:01.710
to keep the faith pure.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:04.900
And so they're starting their own churches.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.780
They're starting colleges, their own publications.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:12.160
We...

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:12.910
Hey.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:16.250
Just one obligation in this group.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.630
That is to run a Christian school.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:22.540
A thing that isn't Christian?

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:24.910
Doesn't belong on this campus.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:29.670
No compromise, no trimming, no cutting corners.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.860
A Christian school, that's our job.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:38.720
That would have been a very strict environment for a Presbyterian Billy Graham.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:44.100
Dating at Bob Jones College had to be scheduled, was restricted.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.380
15 minutes at a time in a

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.850
dormitory parlor, chaperoned.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.930
No touching. Billy couldn't

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:56.360
stand it. He liked playing

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.740
and so this kind of

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.140
The strict demeanor of his parents' house and of Bob Jones really didn't suit him at all.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.580
He did not do well academically either. He flunked math.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.660
He was there for four months, and he was extremely unhappy.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:20.570
Bob Jones was unflinching in his conviction that his opinions were right

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.320
and those who disagreed with him were mistaken.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.980
And he predicted that Billy, when he decided to leave Bob Jones College, was not going to...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:30.920
to amount to anything.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:41.740
Graham decides to go to Florida Bible Institute.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:46.730
He had a friend there who talked about sunshine

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:50.790
and orange trees and a golf course right next door.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:55.530
Florida Bible Institute was theologically conservative,

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:57.030
but it was more relaxed.

00:12:58.840 --> 00:12:59.980
He's still trying to figure out...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:08.520
out who he is. And what he's beginning to learn is that the persona of a preacher suits him very

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:19.300
well. It's well attuned to his gifts and to his abilities. In the South at the time Billy Graham

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:24.840
was growing up, being an evangelist, being a preacher, they were cultural heroes. This was

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:29.630
about as high on the status chart as you could get. He began preaching.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:39.050
And he'd preach in these sort of derelict missions and the scrabbly churches with dogs in the sandy front yard.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:44.030
And as a friend said, he preached to anything that would stand still.

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:51.430
The confidence, the ambition grew when he realized that he was actually really good at preaching.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:59.970
It was when those first people stepped forward and converted to Christ because of his preaching that he got the...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.240
recognition that was so important to keep him going.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:11.570
When Billy Graham graduated, the president noted, Billy just wants to do something big

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:12.230
for God.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:15.210
He's not sure what it is yet, but he wants it to be really big.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:24.900
After he graduates from Florida Bible Institute, he says to himself, I still need more preparation,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:25.500
more training.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:26.800
So he heads off to Wheaton College.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:29.970
He's trying, I think, at that point to accumulate.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.020
a bit of theological ballast.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.880
But the big thing that happens for Billy Graham

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.980
at Wheaton College is that he meets Ruth.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:48.060
Ruth Bell was the daughter

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.330
of a Presbyterian medical missionary

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:54.110
who had led the medical mission in Xinjiang, China.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.180
As a child, she had dreamed of being a missionary

00:14:58.180 --> 00:14:59.800
and hoped to die a martyr.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:15.770
death. She wanted to be a single woman in the plain of Tibet in the thin air by herself preaching

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:29.830
the gospel to nomads. So she wanted to be as solitary, as challenged by a kind of Christian

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:40.850
her as she could be. Billy wrote Mother a letter and told her about Ruth and said,

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:49.830
Mother, I know I'm going to marry her. Ruth was a good student. Pretty much everybody acknowledged

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:57.280
that she was a better student than he was. He told her on the third date that he did not feel

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.220
the call to be a missionary.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:05.070
She eventually surrendered her missionary vocation,

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:09.030
but nobody who knew Ruth Bell Graham

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:11.120
ever thought she surrendered her will.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:37.180
This was a portentous time in American life,

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:40.360
coming out of the Depression and then going into war.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.200
These were years and years of troubled times.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.100
Prior to World War II, what we now know as evangelical Protestantism

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.670
wore the label of fundamentalism.

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:59.980
As the nation mobilizes, many conservative Christians...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.700
relabel themselves as evangelicals.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.260
Youth for Christ was a byproduct

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:10.040
of this emerging new evangelicalism.

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:17.060
Youth for Christ was a more optimistic

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:20.720
conservative Protestantism, much more forward-looking,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.620
something that is in tune with the kind of spirit of the day,

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:28.860
one in which America is emerging from war

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:29.980
and rebuilding itself.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.130
Youth for Christ events resembled a kind of a Christian vaudeville show.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:42.590
Preachers themselves, they would dress very flashily, bright colored suits, bright

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:49.370
ties, and sometimes bow ties that would light up. Their slogan was geared to the

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:53.740
times but anchored to the rock. You don't have to be considered a bumpkin

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:58.610
anymore. You don't have to be considered intellectually retrograde.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:09.000
a part of American life. Billy is drawn in to Youth for Christ as an evangelist. He was offered

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:17.030
a position to be a full-time evangelist working for them. Youth for Christ is for Graham a kind

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:26.500
of halfway house out of the kind of strict, starchy fundamentalism of his childhood into a larger arena.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.960
remember going to Winona Lake, Indiana, where they had the annual Youth for Christ conference

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:44.560
and different preacher every night. Billy was different than the others. The power of that

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:52.570
voice to make people just listen was very striking. Once it says, like a train whistle on a prairie,

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:56.790
we could hear it like a sound of something in the distance, but very, very powerful.

00:18:59.230 --> 00:18:59.910
He loved this.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.710
He liked exciting people and getting them to commit to Christ.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:16.420
He did have that personal ability to sense, I think, people that he spoke with who were open to the message and open to him.

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:28.700
He was incredibly energetic and in three years he went through doing revivals in some 47 states and all Canadian provinces.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:36.550
The audience was never big enough for Graham.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.530
I always had this sense of reaching out,

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.570
have to do more, and find different ways to do it.

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:47.160
Graham comes to a point where he recognizes

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.380
that even Youth for Christ is too confining for him.

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:54.450
And this is when he decides to strike out on his own.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:19:59.560
He chooses, of all places, Los Angeles.

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:05.720
the home of Hollywood, and this is going to be his launching pad into his own.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:06.380
career.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:16.190
has been in this spiritual battle, both internally and with his fellow evangelist and friend Charles

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:16.750
Templeton.

